    "usageNote": "كُلّ is a core Arabic quantifier: prefixed to a noun it means \"all/every\" (كُلُّ رَجُلٍ, \"every man\"), and after a negative plus an exception it narrows to \"any one at all,\" as in the Qur'anic verse cited above. It also forms set compounds — كُلَّمَا (\"whenever, every time that\") and كُلَّمَنْ (\"whoever\") — and doubling the idea intensifies a quality, as in هُوَ العَالِمُ كُلُّ العَالِمِ, \"he is learning itself\" (i.e. supremely learned). The wider root ك-ل-ل otherwise centers on fatigue, dullness/bluntness, and adornment (crowning), branches preserved in related derivations rather than in كُلّ itself."
